  [1mWhat changed from your original request, and why:[0m

  You asked for a notifications system — "notify users when tasks they care about change." What the spec describes is a
  single-user local task app with [1mdue-date reminders[0m. That's a smaller and different thing, and the reasoning is worth
  restating plainly: in a one-person local app you are the only actor, so an event-subscription system would spend all
  its machinery telling you about edits you just made. The passage of time is the only change that happens without you.
  Everything about subscriptions, interest models, and event pipelines dropped out once "single user" was settled.

  [1mThe constraint I'd want you to notice before reading:[0m reminders cannot fire when the browser is closed. That's a
  platform limit, not a design choice — the two APIs that would allow it are Chrome-only and experimental. The spec
  states this rather than working around it. If reminders reaching you with the browser shut is actually a requirement,
  say so now: it invalidates the local-web-app approach entirely, and we'd be talking about a different platform rather
  than a different design.

  Also on the record in the spec's Non-Goals, so they can't drift back in silently: multi-user, sharing, device sync,
  recurrence, activity log, and per-task reminder overrides.

  Please review the spec and let me know if you want changes. Once you approve it, the next step is the writing-plans
  skill to turn it into an implementation plan — still no code until that plan exists.

